 ## Overview
-A Sparse Voxel Tree (SVO) is a hierarchical data structure for representing sparse volumetric data, where the 3D domain is recursively subdivided into octants (nodes with 8 children) [S3, S4, unofficial]. It solves the storage and traversal inefficiency of dense 3D grids by storing samples of occupancy or density fields only in leaf nodes where data exists, allowing ray marching to skip empty space efficiently [S3, unofficial]. Unlike its sibling [Sparse Voxel DAGs](2791-sparse-voxel-dags.md) which merge identical subtrees, the SVO retains a strict tree topology, though it often uses block-based memory layouts to facilitate dynamic updates [S2].
+A Sparse Voxel Tree (SVO) is a hierarchical data structure for representing sparse volumetric data, typically an octree where each node partitions 3D space into 8 children [S3, S4, unofficial]. It solves the storage and traversal inefficiency of dense 3D grids by storing samples of occupancy or density fields only in leaf nodes where data exists, allowing ray marching to skip empty space efficiently [S3, unofficial]. Unlike its sibling [Sparse Voxel DAGs](2791-sparse-voxel-dags.md) which merge identical subtrees to reduce redundancy, the SVO maintains a strict tree topology, relying on pointer-based or block-indexed navigation [S1, unofficial][S2].
 
