 ## Operating characteristics
-*   **Time Complexity:** $O(MAX\_DEPTH)$ for allocation, retrieval, and deallocation. Since `MAX_DEPTH` is constant (3 to 10), operations are effectively $O(1)$ with a small constant factor [S1, unofficial].
+*   **Time Complexity:** $O(\text{MAX\_DEPTH})$ for allocation, lookup, and deallocation. Since `MAX_DEPTH` is constant (3 to 10), operations are effectively $O(1)$ with a small constant factor [S1, unofficial].
 *   **Memory Complexity:** $O(N)$ where $N$ is the number of allocated voxels. Each block holds exactly 8 elements (nodes or voxels). Overhead includes block metadata and free lists [S1, unofficial].
 *   **Measured Performance:** ESVO requires about 5 bytes per voxel with color, normal, and contour, or 6.67 bytes per finest-level sample once the mip hierarchy is counted [S2]. If only surfaces are encoded, memory usage drops to $O(N^2)$ [S2].
-*   **Strengths:** Adapts to the location of scene geometry, storing only leaf nodes where data exists, unlike dense 3D grids which require $4096^3$ cells (~128 GB for rgb$\sigma$) [S3, unofficial]. Octree data in blocks with relative pointers facilitates dynamic updates, such as streaming [S2].
-*   **Limitations:** Octree traversal is worse than hierarchical-grid methods by as much as 60% [S2]. SVOs are considered good only for storage and rendering, and "not even great" at those tasks in some contexts [S2]. MSVC compilation can cause significant branch/pipeline overhead in traversal loops [S1, unofficial].
