 ## Background
-The Sparse Voxel Octree (SVO) is a tree-structure for representing sparse volumetric data, inspired by B+ trees used in databases [S3, unofficial]. It was popularized by OpenVDB [S3, unofficial]. The method allows ray marching to skip empty space efficiently by storing samples of occupancy or density fields in the tree structure instead of triangles [S3, unofficial]. Laine & Karras (2010) presented a sparse voxel octree-based technique for representing geometry and shading attributes, which is cited as a key reference for the ESVO format [S2][S7, unofficial].
+The Sparse Voxel Octree (SVO) is a tree-structure for representing sparse volumetric data, inspired by B+ trees used in databases [S3, unofficial]. It was popularized by Laine & Karras (2010) for efficient ray marching and rendering [S7, unofficial]. The structure partitions 3D space recursively, with interior nodes having no children implying the same value for all children in the subtree [S3, unofficial]. It is widely used in volume rendering to accelerate ray marching by skipping empty space [S3, unofficial].
 
